debug> 

====================================================================
DIAGNOSIS & PROGRESS (2026-07-23, v1.9.3)

Root cause is a CASCADE of three bocl bugs exposed by cl-ppcre's regex
parser/converter.  Loading was traced by loading the component files
directly (packages…scanner…api) interpreted, then reducing:

  1. LOOP `for v = init THEN step` stepping bug  [FIXED v1.9.3]
     When INIT references a PRECEDING loop variable, iteration 1 left V at
     its stale LETB value (NIL), e.g. `(loop for q in '(a b c)
     for s = q then (list s q) finally (return s))` gave ((NIL B) C)
     instead of ((A B) C).  cl-ppcre's PARSER (parser.lisp SEQ) uses exactly
     this shape (`for quant = (quant lexer) ... for seq = quant then (cond…)`),
     so EVERY parse dropped its first token to NIL — "a"→NIL,
     "ab"→(:SEQUENCE NIL #\b) — which then hit CONVERT-SIMPLE-PARSE-TREE's
     default method → "Undefined function PARSE-TREE-SYNONYM" (the reported
     symptom; PARSE-TREE-SYNONYM is defined later in api.lisp).
     Fix: %loop-expand now assigns a standalone `= init then step`'s INIT in
     SETUPS on the first iteration (after preceding FOR clauses step), not in
     the LET.  (prelude.lisp)

  2. COERCE of an adjustable char vector to STRING  [FIXED v1.9.3]
     `(coerce <adjustable-char-vector> 'simple-string)` raised
     "expected VECTOR, got %MD-ARRAY" because the vector→string / vector→list
     branches used SVREF/VECTOR-LENGTH (simple-vector only).  Fixed to use
     ELT/LENGTH.  (prelude.lisp)

  3. Adjustable / fill-pointer character vectors are not STRINGs  [OPEN]
     After 1 & 2, parsing is fully correct and (SCAN …) works, but api.lisp
     still fails building the \Q-quoting scanners: a multi-character literal
     run (e.g. the literal \E) is accumulated by the parser into an
     ADJUSTABLE string, which bocl classifies as %MD-ARRAY.  STRINGP / TYPEP
     'STRING are false for it, so CONVERT-SIMPLE-PARSE-TREE's ((x string))
     method is not selected (CLOS dispatch tests built-in specializers via
     TYPEP) and it falls to the default → "Unknown token \E in parse tree."
     This is a type-system-level defect filed separately as
     adjustable-string-not-string-class.issue — the remaining blocker.  It is
     a larger change (unify complex 1-D char arrays with STRING across
     STRINGP/TYPEP/CLASS-OF and the string kernel primitives), so cl-ppcre
     stays OPEN until it lands (further bugs may surface past api.lisp once it
     does).

VERIFIED after 1 & 2: all cl-ppcre parse trees correct
(e.g. "a.c"→(:SEQUENCE #\a :EVERYTHING #\c), "x*?"→(:NON-GREEDY-REPETITION
0 NIL #\x)); (cl-ppcre:scan "a.c" "xabcy") => 1 4 #() #().

--------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE (v1.9.4, 2026-07-24)
Bug 3 (adjustable-string-not-string-class) is FIXED — the type system now
recognises complex character arrays as STRINGs and CLOS dispatches the
((parse-tree string)) method correctly; CHAR/SCHAR/SUBSEQ etc. work on them.
cl-ppcre now loads further and (cl-ppcre:scan "a.c" "xabcy") works.  The next
blocker is unrelated: api.lisp uses (SETF (THE type place) value) and bocl's
SETF has no handler for the THE place operator ("SETF: no expander for place
operator THE").  Filed as setf-the-place.issue.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE (v1.9.5, 2026-07-24)
Fixed SETF of THE and of macro places (setf-the-place.issue) — cl-ppcre now
LOADS COMPLETELY and (scan …), (do-matches …), (do-matches-as-strings …) all
work.  Next blocker: the aggregating functions (all-matches-as-strings,
regex-replace-all, split) pass a pre-compiled scanner CLOSURE to the generic
function SCAN, whose ((scanner function)) method is not selected because the
kernel TYPEP does not see a closure as a FUNCTION.  Filed as
typep-function-closure.issue.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE (v1.9.6, 2026-07-24) — INTERPRETER SIDE DONE
The whole interpreter cascade is fixed (LOOP `= then`, COERCE %MD-ARRAY,
adjustable-string type system, SETF of THE + macro places, closure-as-FUNCTION).
Loading cl-ppcre from source with LOAD works and SCAN / REGEX-REPLACE-ALL /
ALL-MATCHES-AS-STRINGS / SPLIT all give correct results.
REMAINING: the original (ql:quickload …) path COMPILE-FILEs the sources and
SIGSEGVs while compiling lexer.lisp — a separate compiler/cfasl bug, filed as
cl-ppcre-compile-file-segfault.issue.  Keep this open until that is fixed.
